---
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro'
import Preview from '~/components/Preview.astro'
import PropsTable from '~/components/PropsTable.astro'
import FeedbackDemo from '~/demos/feedback-demo'
---

<DocsShell
  title="Feedback"
  description="Alerts, toasts, and the three states every asynchronous region needs."
>
  <Preview demo="feedback-demo" title="Alerts, toasts and states" contained={false}>
    <FeedbackDemo client:visible />
  </Preview>

  <h2 id="alert">Alert</h2>
  <p>
    A persistent, inline message about the surrounding content. <code>warning</code> and
    <code>danger</code> are announced assertively; the rest are polite. For transient feedback about something
    the user just did, use a toast.
  </p>
  <PropsTable type="AlertProps" element="div" />

  <h2 id="toast">Toast</h2>
  <p>
    Mount <code>ToastProvider</code> once near the app root and <code>Toaster</code> once inside it.
    <code>useToast().promise()</code> wires a pending, success and error toast to an async action in one
    call.
  </p>
  <p>
    Toasts render with <code>role="dialog"</code> — the ARIA pattern for a notification that can contain
    actions. Worth knowing when writing tests: address dialogs by their accessible name.
  </p>
  <PropsTable type="ToasterProps" />

  <h2 id="states">Empty, loading and error</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If a list is empty because a filter excluded everything, say so and offer to clear it. Telling
      someone to "create your first item" when they have fifty is the classic version of this bug.
    </li>
    <li>
      Prefer <code>Skeleton</code> over <code>LoadingState</code> when you know the shape of what is coming
      — it avoids the layout shift that a spinner cannot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>ErrorState</code> shows a generic message to the user and the real error in development. Swallowing
      the message is how a five minute bug becomes an afternoon.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PropsTable type="ErrorStateProps" element="div" />
</DocsShell>
